    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где мой сушист?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is my sushi chef?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'where' directly with 'sushi chef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Откуда я знаю?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How should I know?' Here Senya anchors the line on the word meaning 'where from' and builds the thought around 'know'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Утром он был здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He was here this morning.' The phrasing opens with 'this morning' before bringing in 'was' and 'here' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Может, он заболел.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe he got sick.' The verb for 'to fall ill' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Суши отменяются?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is sushi canceled?' Kristina turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'sushi', followed by 'to be canceled'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего не отменяется.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nothing is canceled.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'is canceled', which directs the action back onto the subject. Then Kristina responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "У нас восемь заказов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have eight orders.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'at' directly with 'orders'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я сам всё сделаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will do everything myself.' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'I'll do'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Клиенты ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Customers are waiting.' Kristina puts the word for 'client' together with 'are waiting' in a crisp, natural spoken turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Идите отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Get out of here.' Chef Barinov leads with the direct command form meaning 'come', followed by 'from here'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Мы уже уходим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are already leaving.' The phrasing opens with 'already' before bringing in 'are leaving' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Думай быстрее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Think fast.' Kristina leads with the direct command form meaning 'think', followed by 'faster'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
